# Playwright conventions

Button names, labels, and routes below are illustrative. P7 confirms real BuddyTime strings from the live app.

1. Locators — `getByRole`, then label or placeholder, then text, then test id; CSS and XPath are review smells.
2. Waiting — web-first `expect()` only; never `waitForTimeout` or `expect(await locator.isVisible())`.
3. Page Object Model — one class per page or component; locators in the constructor; methods are user actions; no assertions.
4. Auth — log in once per family in the setup project; reuse `AUTH_FILE` and `ALT_AUTH_FILE`; credentials from `process.env`.
5. Test data — unique per test, cleaned up by that test, order-independent, never another student's data.
6. Tags — exactly one tag on each `test()`; never on `describe()`; `@destructive` is only shared/global state.
7. Test data sources — Faker factories, static invalid sets, `process.env`, and route enums.
8. API — the `request` fixture for setup, teardown, and contracts; skip the UI when an API exists.
9. Network mocking — `page.route` / `route.fulfill` for failures and empty payloads; never mock the endpoint under test.
10. Accessibility — axe `wcag2a`/`wcag2aa` on key pages, plus a keyboard check; `getByRole(name)` proves the accessible name.
11. Assertions — web-first by default; `expect.soft` for several checks; screenshots only with dynamic regions masked.
12. Anti-flake — CI retries, isolation, `page.clock`, pinned locale and timezone, trace on first retry; `retries: 5` or `workers: 1` are not fixes.

## 1. Locators

Prefer `getByRole`, then `getByLabel` or `getByPlaceholder`, then `getByText`, then `getByTestId`. A CSS or XPath locator is a smell flagged for review.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();
await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(email);
await page.getByPlaceholder('Search').fill('widget');
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
await page.locator('button.primary').click();
await page.locator('//form/input[1]').fill(email);
```

Why: role, label, and placeholder locators follow what a user can see and survive layout changes.

## 2. Waiting

Wait with a web-first `expect()`. Never call `waitForTimeout`, and never snapshot a boolean with `expect(await locator.isVisible())`.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
await expect(page.getByRole('status')).toBeVisible();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
await page.waitForTimeout(1000);
expect(await page.getByRole('status').isVisible()).toBe(true);
```

Why: web-first `expect` retries until the condition is true; a fixed sleep and a one-shot boolean do not.

## 3. Page Object Model

One class per page or component. Declare locators in the constructor, and make methods user actions. Do not put assertions in a page object.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
export class WidgetPage {
  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {}

  private readonly save = this.page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' });

  /** Saves the widget the user just edited. */
  async saveWidget(): Promise<void> {
    await this.save.click();
  }
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
export class WidgetPage {
  async saveWidget(page: Page): Promise<void> {
    await expect(page.locator('#save')).toBeEnabled();
    await page.locator('#save').click();
  }
}
```

Why: the test owns the assertion; the page object only performs the action a user would.

## 4. Auth

Log in once per family in the setup project and save `storageState`: `AUTH_FILE` for the main family, `ALT_AUTH_FILE` for the second. A two-family test opens a second context with `browser.newContext({ storageState: ALT_AUTH_FILE })`. Read credentials from `process.env`. Never log in inside each test.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
import { AUTH_FILE, ALT_AUTH_FILE } from '../support/auth.constants';

setup('authenticate main family', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(process.env.APP_USER_EMAIL ?? '');
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill(process.env.APP_USER_PASSWORD ?? '');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
  await page.context().storageState({ path: AUTH_FILE });
});

test('both families see the widget', { tag: '@e2e' }, async ({ browser, page }) => {
  const alt = await browser.newContext({ storageState: ALT_AUTH_FILE });
  const altPage = await alt.newPage();
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Widgets' })).toBeVisible();
  await expect(altPage.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Widgets' })).toBeVisible();
  await alt.close();
});
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
test('saves a widget', { tag: '@smoke' }, async ({ page }) => {
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('user@example.test');
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('replace-with-password');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
});
```

Why: one stored session per family keeps credentials out of tests and avoids logging in on every run.

## 5. Test data

Each test creates unique data with a factory or `Date.now()`, then cleans that data up. Tests must not depend on order or on another student's data in the shared environment.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
const name = `Widget ${Date.now()}`;
const created = await request.post('/api/widgets', { data: { name } });
const id = (await created.json()).id;
try {
  await expect(page.getByRole('cell', { name })).toBeVisible();
} finally {
  await request.delete(`/api/widgets/${id}`);
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
test('sees the shared widget', async ({ page }) => {
  await expect(page.getByRole('cell', { name: 'Homework widget' })).toBeVisible();
});
```

Why: unique, self-owned rows can run in parallel and do not collide with anyone else's data.

## 6. Tags

Put exactly one tag on each `test()`: `@smoke` (every PR), `@sanity` (every push), `@regression` (full suite on manual dispatch), `@api`, `@e2e`, or `@destructive`. `@destructive` is only for shared or global state both families depend on, such as account settings, and that run uses `--workers=1`. Never tag `describe()`. A test that only touches data it created is not `@destructive`. Any test that mutates state reverts it in `afterEach` or `afterAll`.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
test('saves a widget', { tag: '@smoke' }, async ({ page }) => {
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('status')).toBeVisible();
});
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
test.describe('widgets', { tag: '@smoke' }, () => {
  test('saves a widget @sanity', async ({ page }) => {});
});
```

Why: one tag per test selects the right pipeline, and a describe-level tag would mark every test inside it.

## 7. Test data sources

Build fresh payloads in `test-data/factories/` with Faker and `Date.now()`. Keep static invalid sets in `test-data/*.ts`. Read secrets and URLs from `process.env`. Put repeated strings in enums such as `test-data/routes.ts`.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
import { Routes } from '../test-data/routes';
import { widget } from '../test-data/factories/widget';

await page.goto(Routes.widgets);
await request.post('/api/widgets', { data: widget() });
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
await page.goto('/widgets');
await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('user@example.test');
```

Why: factories and enums stay unique and consistent; secrets never live in source.

## 8. API

Use the `request` fixture for setup, teardown, and contract checks. Do not click through the UI to create a prerequisite when an API exists.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
test('returns the created widget', { tag: '@api' }, async ({ request }) => {
  const response = await request.post('/api/widgets', { data: widget() });
  expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy();
  await request.delete(`/api/widgets/${(await response.json()).id}`);
});
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
test('returns the created widget', { tag: '@api' }, async ({ page, request }) => {
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();
  const response = await request.get('/api/widgets');
  expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy();
});
```

Why: API setup is faster and does not depend on screens that are not under test.

## 9. Network mocking

Use `page.route` and `route.fulfill` for a 500, a 503, a timeout, an empty list, or a malformed payload. Never mock the endpoint the test is actually checking.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
await page.route('**/api/widgets', (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 500, body: '' }),
);
await expect(page.getByRole('alert')).toBeVisible();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
test('creates a widget', { tag: '@api' }, async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/api/widgets', (route) =>
    route.fulfill({ status: 201, body: '{"id":"1"}' }),
  );
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();
});
```

Why: mocking a failure shows how the UI handles it; mocking the call under test hides whether that call works.

## 10. Accessibility

Scan key pages with `@axe-core/playwright` for `wcag2a` and `wcag2aa`, and check that the page is operable from the keyboard. A working `getByRole` locator with an accessible name is evidence that the control has that name.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page }).withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa']).analyze();
expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
await page.keyboard.press('Tab');
await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' })).toBeFocused();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
await page.locator('#save').click();
```

Why: the axe tags catch WCAG failures, and the keyboard check proves a user can reach the control without a pointer.

## 11. Assertions

Assert with web-first `expect` by default. Use `expect.soft` when one test checks several independent facts. Use `toHaveScreenshot` only with dynamic regions masked.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
await expect(page.getByRole('status')).toHaveText('Saved');
await expect.soft(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Widgets' })).toBeVisible();
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot({ mask: [page.getByText(/\d{1,2}:\d{2}/)] });
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
expect(await page.getByRole('status').textContent()).toBe('Saved');
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot();
```

Why: web-first assertions retry, soft checks report every failure, and a mask keeps clocks and other live text out of the snapshot.

## 12. Anti-flake

Retry in CI only, isolate every test, and use `page.clock` where availability or calendars depend on "now". Locale and timezone stay pinned, and a trace is captured on the first retry. `playwright.config.ts` already does this with `retries` only when `CI` is set, `locale: 'en-CA'`, `timezoneId: 'America/Toronto'`, and `trace: 'on-first-retry'`. Raising `retries` to 5 or setting `workers` to 1 is not a fix.

```typescript
// ✅ Good
await page.clock.setFixedTime(new Date('2026-09-28T15:00:00'));
await expect(page.getByRole('gridcell', { name: '28' })).toBeVisible();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
export default defineConfig({
  retries: 5,
  workers: 1,
});
```

Why: a pinned clock and an isolated test remove the race; extra retries and a single worker only hide it.

## Refusals (hard stops)

These are conflicts the agent raises. They are not preferences that can be talked out of.

- Never weaken or delete an assertion to make a test pass.
- Never heal a real product bug in the test.
- Never replace a role locator with CSS or XPath.
- Never use `waitForTimeout`, a hardcoded credential, or the `any` type.
